From: SynQt <https://synqt.org/>
Subject: [PATCH] wasm: send posted events from the native timer callback too

QCoreApplication::postEvent() reaches QEventDispatcherWasm::wakeUp(), which on the
main thread schedules delivery by arming a zero-delay QWasmTimer, from inside an
emscripten_async_call() which is itself a zero-delay browser callback. That two-hop
chain is the only thing that ever calls QCoreApplication::sendPostedEvents() on the
main thread, and wakeUp() runs when an event is posted, not again while it waits. So
if the browser drops or starves either hop, nothing re-arms the delivery and the
posted event is never sent again - not late, never.

The application does not look dead while that happens. Qt timers ride a different
QWasmTimer, armed with a real interval, and QTimerInfoList::activateTimers() delivers
with sendEvent(), so they keep firing. Sockets keep reading, because their callbacks
are DOM events. Anything whose only completion path is a queued signal, though, is
lost, and the caller is never told: the case that surfaced this is a returning
QtRemoteObjects slot, whose reply arrives, decodes, and sets error and returnValue on
the call synchronously, while QRemoteObjectPendingCallWatcher::finished - a
Qt::QueuedConnection, so a posted QEvent::MetaCall - never arrives. Observed in
Firefox on GitHub's hosted Ubuntu runner, with Chromium and WebKit passing on the same
runner and Firefox passing everywhere else.

Sending posted events from onTimer() as well gives that delivery a second, independent
way to happen. It costs one sendPostedEvents() sweep per native timer callback, and it
turns a permanently undelivered event into one delayed by at most a timer interval.

Reproducing it does not need the runner. Drop the zero-delay browser timeout that
wakeUp() arms - and only that one, leaving the native Qt timer alone - and every
engine shows the same stall; with this patch every engine recovers. The harness is
tests/m0-transport/verify/verify-pump.mjs in the SynQt repository.

--- a/src/corelib/kernel/qeventdispatcher_wasm.cpp
+++ b/src/corelib/kernel/qeventdispatcher_wasm.cpp
@@ -476,6 +476,22 @@
     if (useAsyncify())
         return;
 
+    // Send posted events as well, not just timer events. wakeUp() is the only code path
+    // which schedules posted event delivery, and it does so by arming a zero-delay native
+    // timer, via an emscripten_async_call() which is itself a zero-delay callback. wakeUp()
+    // is called when an event is posted and not again while that event waits, so if either
+    // of those two callbacks is dropped or starved by the browser, nothing re-arms the
+    // delivery and the posted event is never sent - permanently, even though the
+    // application is otherwise healthy and its timers keep firing, since
+    // QTimerInfoList::activateTimers() delivers with sendEvent() and does not need the
+    // posted event queue. Sending posted events here costs one sweep per timer callback
+    // and bounds that failure to a single timer interval instead of forever.
+    g_mainThreadEventDispatcher->sendPostedEvents();
+
+    // sendPostedEvents() runs application code, which may have deleted the event dispatcher.
+    if (!g_mainThreadEventDispatcher)
+        return;
+
     g_mainThreadEventDispatcher->sendTimerEvents();
 }
 
